WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.890
<v Instructor>Okay, so now let's talk about Aurora,</v>

2
00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:03.810
which is going to be a big focus on the exam

3
00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:04.890
from an architectural point-of-view.

4
00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.060
Again, to understand the difference

5
00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:09.840
between Aurora RDS and DynamoDB, for example.

6
00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.820
So Aurora is only for PostgreSQL and MySQL-compatible APIs,

7
00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.610
and the really cool thing about Aurora is the storage.

8
00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:21.690
So it automatically grows by increments of 10 gigabytes

9
00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:23.580
up to 256 terabytes.

10
00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.580
So storage is something you don't have to think about

11
00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:29.760
when using Aurora, it's one less constraints on your end.

12
00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:33.300
There's six copies of data being made across different AZ,

13
00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.960
and Aurora by default is going to be a multi-AZ database.

14
00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:37.920
In terms of read replicas,

15
00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.680
you can create up to 15 read replicas

16
00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:43.320
and you're going to get a reader endpoint to access them all

17
00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:44.400
so you don't need to remember

18
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:45.990
where all your read replicas are.

19
00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:48.570
You can just use them from a reader endpoint.

20
00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:50.640
You can do cross region read replica,

21
00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.080
in which case you need to know

22
00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.750
that the entire database is going to be copied,

23
00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:56.250
not just select tables.

24
00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.620
So this is different from DynamoDB.

25
00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:00.780
So if you need to replicate just a single table

26
00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.780
that you need to create a specific database for that.

27
00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:05.290
With Cross-region Replica,

28
00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:08.160
it is the entire database that's being copied.

29
00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:13.160
And you can have the capability to load or export data

30
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.630
directly from or to S3.

31
00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:18.210
The idea is that you don't need to use a client application

32
00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:19.830
to read the data from Aurora

33
00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:22.410
and then add it into S3, or vice versa.

34
00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:25.230
It goes directly from Aurora into S3,

35
00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:29.670
hence saving you a lot of resources and network costs.

36
00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:31.860
There's backup, snapshots, and resource feature,

37
00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.320
which are the exact same as RDS.

38
00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.810
Okay, now let's have a look at how Aurora works

39
00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:38.460
from an architectural standpoint.

40
00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:41.523
So you get six copies of your data across 3 AZ,

41
00:01:42.916 --> 00:01:45.600
and four out of the six copies need are needed for writes,

42
00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.690
and three out of the six copies are needed for reads.

43
00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:51.060
There's self-healing with peer-to-peer replication,

44
00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:52.020
so you don't need to worry about it.

45
00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:55.290
This is Amazon's Web Services engineering behind this

46
00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:56.670
that made it possible,

47
00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:58.950
and the storage of your database is going to be striped

48
00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:00.600
across hundreds of volume,

49
00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.640
so there's no single point of failure.

50
00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:03.840
So here's the idea.

51
00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.130
We have a shared storage volume

52
00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:06.974
that is replicated, self-healing, and auto expanding,

53
00:02:06.974 --> 00:02:10.650
and so as as you start inserting data

54
00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:11.913
into your Aurora database,

55
00:02:11.913 --> 00:02:15.270
it's going to be replicated across six blocks

56
00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:16.770
in three different AZ.

57
00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:18.810
So here's the blue, here's the orange,

58
00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:20.700
and here's your entire database.

59
00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:23.040
Then you're gonna get different Aurora there.

60
00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:24.561
So in case you have a master,

61
00:02:24.561 --> 00:02:27.018
you need to know that the failover for the master happens

62
00:02:27.018 --> 00:02:29.100
in less than 30 seconds,

63
00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:29.970
so this is quite helpful,

64
00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:32.299
and the master is the only database in Aurora

65
00:02:32.299 --> 00:02:35.400
that's able to write to the storage.

66
00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.140
And then you can use the master

67
00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.833
plus up to 15 Aurora Read Replicas to serve the reads.

68
00:02:39.833 --> 00:02:43.650
So you can add as many as the, for example,

69
00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:47.010
six or five read replicas in this example

70
00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.200
to help you enhance the read volume.

71
00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:50.175
So the idea here is

72
00:02:50.175 --> 00:02:53.550
that you can really have the RDS capability

73
00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:55.980
while making sure it's going to be a bit more cloud native,

74
00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:57.930
a bit more fast for failovers,

75
00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:00.450
in case there's an issue with the master.

76
00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.591
Now finally, as I said, there is support

77
00:03:02.591 --> 00:03:05.520
for cross-region replication for your read replica.

78
00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:07.596
So this could be a way for you

79
00:03:07.596 --> 00:03:09.060
to create a disaster recovery strategy,

80
00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:12.630
and then you would promote that replica into its own region

81
00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:16.710
in case there is a global outage in your region itself.

82
00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:18.780
Okay, so for your DB cluster,

83
00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:21.900
we have this shared storage that has autoexpanding,

84
00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:23.490
and we have a writer endpoint.

85
00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:24.750
This is going to point directly

86
00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:27.270
into the master Aurora database,

87
00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:29.970
and then the client, if it talks to the write endpoint,

88
00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:31.830
will be redirected to that master,

89
00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:33.810
and then we have a bunch of read replicas

90
00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:35.850
and they could be autoscaling as well,

91
00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:36.690
so it's possible for you

92
00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:39.420
to add and remove read replicas over time.

93
00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:42.120
And the reader endpoint is going to be used

94
00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:44.220
for doing a connection load balancing.

95
00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:46.380
And so the client should use the reader endpoint

96
00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:47.790
to make sure it's gonna be reading

97
00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:51.090
from one of the read replicas in Aurora.

98
00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:53.010
Okay, next, let's talk about Aurora endpoints.

99
00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:55.650
So an endpoint is the combination of a host address

100
00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:58.140
and a port, and you have multiple types of endpoints.

101
00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.570
You have a cluster endpoint that's a writer endpoint,

102
00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:02.190
which allows you to connect

103
00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:05.040
to the primary DB instance in your Aurora cluster,

104
00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:07.170
that is going to be used for all the right operations.

105
00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:11.130
So inserts, update deletes, as well as maybe some queries.

106
00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:12.180
Then we have the reader endpoint.

107
00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:14.370
And the reader endpoint is going to provide load balancing

108
00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:15.990
for read-only connections

109
00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:19.380
to all the Aurora replicas in your Aurora cluster.

110
00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:21.090
So it's going to be used only for read operations,

111
00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:22.230
so queries.

112
00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:24.510
And then you can define custom endpoints

113
00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:27.120
and these endpoints are a set of database instances

114
00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:29.160
that you choose in the Aurora cluster,

115
00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.140
and they're very helpful when you want to connect

116
00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:33.600
to different of database instances

117
00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:35.640
with different capacities and configurations,

118
00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:38.670
for example, a different DB parameter group.

119
00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:40.440
And finally, you have an instance endpoint,

120
00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:41.760
which is the endpoint to connect

121
00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:44.700
to a specific database instance in the Aurora cluster,

122
00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:45.533
which is used

123
00:04:45.533 --> 00:04:48.030
when you want to diagnoses a specific DB instance

124
00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:51.123
or fine tune one specific DB instance.

125
00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:53.610
So for custom endpoints, how they work.

126
00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:56.670
So here is represented the writer endpoint

127
00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:58.860
as well as the reader endpoints, okay.

128
00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:02.430
But say you want to define a subset of Aurora instances

129
00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:03.630
as a custom endpoint,

130
00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:05.760
in which case you can define a custom endpoint.

131
00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:08.970
And as you can see, this endpoint is connected

132
00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:12.826
to db.r5.2 x large instances of Amazon Aurora

133
00:05:12.826 --> 00:05:13.890
and I have two.

134
00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:15.660
So there are more powerful instances,

135
00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:18.090
and therefore maybe I wanna use this custom endpoint

136
00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:21.390
to run analytical queries on specific replicas.

137
00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:25.377
So this would be one of the use cases of this feature.

138
00:05:25.377 --> 00:05:27.870
Now the reader endpoint is generally not used

139
00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:29.451
once you define custom endpoints because well,

140
00:05:29.451 --> 00:05:30.960
you would define custom endpoints

141
00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:32.673
for all your database instances.

142
00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:36.210
Next, you have Aurora logs,

143
00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:39.030
so you can monitor the following logs with Aurora logs.

144
00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:41.867
You can define and monitor error logs, slow query logs,

145
00:05:41.867 --> 00:05:45.180
general log, and the audit log.

146
00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:46.710
So all these log files are created

147
00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:49.080
within Aurora and then they can either be downloaded

148
00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:50.733
or published to CloudWatch logs.

149
00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:53.806
So you will find them as a stream in CloudWatch logs

150
00:05:53.806 --> 00:05:54.930
or download them directly on your computer

151
00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:56.700
for offline analysis.

152
00:05:56.700 --> 00:05:58.770
So if you had to troubleshoot the RDS

153
00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:01.170
and the Aurora performance, how would you do it?

154
00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:03.600
Well, you can use the performance insights tool.

155
00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:06.600
This is going to give you issues in your database happening.

156
00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:09.750
So waits, SQL statements, host and users.

157
00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:10.920
To make it more simple,

158
00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:12.660
for example, if you use performance insights

159
00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:14.610
for SQL statements, that means you're going

160
00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:17.340
to see right now the SQL queries are going

161
00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:19.290
to put some load on your database

162
00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:21.540
and you can have a look at them in real time

163
00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:22.890
or for users, the users,

164
00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:24.510
they're actually doing the most load

165
00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:25.650
on your database as well.

166
00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:26.483
To see, for example,

167
00:06:26.483 --> 00:06:29.190
if a application is misbehaving.

168
00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:30.023
The host is going

169
00:06:30.023 --> 00:06:31.410
to give you information around

170
00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:34.200
which servers are using your database the most,

171
00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:36.210
and the waits give you the amount

172
00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:39.000
of time your database spent on computations

173
00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:40.440
or on networking and sort of thing,

174
00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:43.590
so it's lower level type of information.

175
00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:45.330
CloudWatch metrics give you basic information

176
00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:47.400
around the CPU usage, the memory usage

177
00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:49.770
as well, the swap memory usage,.

178
00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:51.570
and enhance monitoring can be enabled

179
00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:53.520
for your RDS database as well.

180
00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:55.200
So you get a lot more information.

181
00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:56.820
You get per second metric.

182
00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:59.490
You get host level information such as, for example,

183
00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:02.060
the top 100 processes running on your database.

184
00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:03.900
So this is really when you wanna go deep

185
00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:06.960
into the process level stuff on your database.

186
00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:09.690
And, of course, if you had to troubleshoot performance,

187
00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:12.570
you can also look at the slow query logs.

188
00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:14.340
Okay, so that's it for this lecture.

189
00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:17.290
I hope you liked it and I will see you in the next lecture.

